export default {"X000107":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>Ultrafilter points of a countable product of binary fields</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000550\"><span>Monomorphism</span><code>P000550</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000002\"><span>Locally of finite type</span><code>P000002</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000107\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000107</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000779\"><span>Source size at most target size</span><code>P000779</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000022\"><span>Quasi-compact</span><code>P000022</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000001\"><span>Locally of finite presentation</span><code>P000001</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Morphisms of schemes</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> Take <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℕ</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">𝔽</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=\\prod_{n\\in\\mathbb N}\\mathbb F_2</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>, <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y=\\operatorname{Spec}B</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>, and <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> the disjoint union of one copy of <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">𝔽</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Spec}\\mathbb F_2</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> for each ultrafilter on <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb N</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>. Each component maps by the quotient associated to that ultrafilter. Distinct kernels are comaximal and each individual diagonal is an isomorphism, so <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X\\to Y</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> is monic and locally of finite type. The <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><msup><mn>2</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℵ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^{2^{\\aleph_0}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> disjoint affine components already exceed <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">size</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℵ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{size}(Y)=2^{\\aleph_0}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>. The two positive cases of 04VA exclude quasi-compactness and local finite presentation.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/04VA\">Stacks 04VA · Stacks Project source · Stacks 04VA · final assertion and ultrafilter counterexample</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/sets/#tag-04VA\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://utheses.univie.ac.at/thesis/55563\">W. Poiger, Independent and almost disjoint families, Proposition 1.15 (number of ultrafilters on the natural numbers)</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000107\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000107\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000112":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>Singleton components of Cantor space</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000054\"><span>Totally disconnected space</span><code>P000054</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000053\"><span>Hausdorff space</span><code>P000053</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000112\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000112</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000843\"><span>Open connected components</span><code>P000843</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Topological spaces</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> In the countable product of the two-point discrete space, distinct sequences differ at a coordinate and its two clopen cylinders separate them. Thus every component is a singleton. A basic neighbourhood fixes only finitely many coordinates, so no singleton is open.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/004T\">Stacks 004T · numbered source statement · Stacks 004T · (2), failure of openness in the proof</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topology/#tag-004T\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://topology.pi-base.org/spaces/S000026\">Pi-Base S000026 · Cantor space</a> · <a href=\"https://topology.pi-base.org/properties/P000234\">Pi-Base P000234 · Has open connected components</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000112\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000112\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000117":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>Isolated sequence with two limits</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000059\"><span>T₀ space</span><code>P000059</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000054\"><span>Totally disconnected space</span><code>P000054</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000117\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000117</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000954\"><span>Components agree with quasicomponents</span><code>P000954</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Topological spaces</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> Add two points to a countable discrete sequence. Basic neighbourhoods of each new point consist of it and a tail. Each old singleton is clopen, and the two new points form a discrete subspace. Thus every connected subset is a singleton. A clopen containing one new point must contain a tail and therefore its other limit. The quasicomponent of either new point contains both.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0FIY\">Stacks 0FIY · numbered source statement · Stacks 0FIY · example of strict containment</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topology/#tag-0FIY\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000117\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000117\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000118":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>Indiscrete spaces with at least two points</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000862\"><span>Quasi-sober space</span><code>P000862</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000118\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000118</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000059\"><span>T₀ space</span><code>P000059</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000058\"><span>Sober space</span><code>P000058</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Topological spaces</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> In every indiscrete space of cardinality at least two, the entire space is its unique nonempty irreducible closed subset and every point is generic. No open distinguishes two points.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0B32\">Stacks 0B32 · numbered source statement · Stacks 0B32 · quasi-sobriety without uniqueness</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topology/#tag-0B32\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://topology.pi-base.org/spaces/S000004\">Pi-Base S000004 · Two-point indiscrete space, a finite instance</a> · <a href=\"https://assets.cambridge.org/97811071/46723/excerpt/9781107146723_excerpt.pdf\">Dickmann, Schwartz and Tressl, Spectral Spaces, §1.1.13 (point closures and uniqueness)</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000118\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000118\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000119":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>Infinite cofinite spaces</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000059\"><span>T₀ space</span><code>P000059</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000859\"><span>Irreducible topological space</span><code>P000859</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000057\"><span>T₁ space</span><code>P000057</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000119\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000119</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000862\"><span>Quasi-sober space</span><code>P000862</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000058\"><span>Sober space</span><code>P000058</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Topological spaces</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> In an infinite cofinite space all singleton sets are closed, while any two nonempty opens intersect. The irreducible closed subset consisting of the whole space therefore has no generic point.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0B33\">Stacks 0B33 · numbered source statement · Stacks 0B33 · Kolmogorov without quasi-sobriety</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topology/#tag-0B33\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://topology.pi-base.org/spaces/S000015\">Pi-Base S000015 · Countable cofinite space, a countable instance</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000119\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000119\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000120":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>Disjoint sum of indiscrete and infinite cofinite spaces</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000561\"><span>Nonempty space</span><code>P000561</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000120\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000120</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000059\"><span>T₀ space</span><code>P000059</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000862\"><span>Quasi-sober space</span><code>P000862</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Topological spaces</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> Take the topological sum of an indiscrete space with at least two points and an infinite cofinite space. Two points of the first cannot be distinguished. The second summand is closed irreducible without a generic point, since its points are closed and points of the first summand have closure disjoint from it.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0B34\">Stacks 0B34 · numbered source statement · Stacks 0B34 · both failures</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topology/#tag-0B34\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://topology.pi-base.org/spaces/S000015\">Pi-Base S000015 · Countable cofinite space, a countable instance</a> · <a href=\"https://topology.pi-base.org/spaces/S000004\">Pi-Base S000004 · Two-point indiscrete space, a finite instance</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000120\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000120\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000121":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>Generic point added to a cofinite space</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000058\"><span>Sober space</span><code>P000058</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000121\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000121</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000955\"><span>Hereditarily quasi-sober space</span><code>P000955</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Topological spaces</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> Fix a point in an infinite set. Take as closed sets the entire space and the finite subsets avoiding that point. The irreducible closed sets are the whole space and its closed singletons, each with a unique generic point. Removing the distinguished point gives an infinite cofinite space with no generic point.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0B35\">Stacks 0B35 · numbered source statement · Stacks 0B35 · sober space with non-quasi-sober subspace</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topology/#tag-0B35\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000121\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000121\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000122":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>Positive integers with finite initial segments open</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000561\"><span>Nonempty space</span><code>P000561</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000059\"><span>T₀ space</span><code>P000059</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000067\"><span>Locally Noetherian space</span><code>P000067</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000122\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000122</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000957\"><span>Existence of a closed point</span><code>P000957</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000063\"><span>Noetherian topological space</span><code>P000063</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Topological spaces</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> Take as opens the empty set, all finite initial segments of the positive integers, and the whole set. Finite initial segments are Noetherian neighbourhoods distinguishing points. Every singleton closure is an infinite final segment and these form a strictly descending sequence.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02HZ\">Stacks 02HZ · numbered source statement · Stacks 02HZ · locally Noetherian space without closed points</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topology/#tag-02HZ\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://wstein.org/sga/ega1.pdf\">Grothendieck and Dieudonné, EGA I, 2nd ed., 0(I), 2.2.3, p. 55</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000122\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000122\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000123":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>Noetherian interval with unbounded irreducible chains</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000063\"><span>Noetherian topological space</span><code>P000063</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000123\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000123</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000960\"><span>Finite codimension of irreducible intervals</span><code>P000960</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000962\"><span>Catenary topological space</span><code>P000962</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Topological spaces</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> Give the unit interval the topology with nonempty proper opens <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>n</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1-1/n,1]</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> for positive integers <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>. Its closed sets form a chain, so every nonempty closed set is irreducible. A strictly descending closed chain must terminate once it leaves the whole interval. From the origin singleton to the whole interval one can instead form ascending chains of arbitrarily large finite length.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02I5\">Stacks 02I5 · numbered source statement · Stacks 02I5 · infinite codimension of the origin in the whole space</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topology/#tag-02I5\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000123\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000123\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000111":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>An fpqc singleton beyond fppf</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000832\"><span>fpqc covering family</span><code>P000832</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000111\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000111</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000830\"><span>fppf covering family</span><code>P000830</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Families of morphisms with a common target</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> Let <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> be a field. The singleton <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Spec}k(t)\\to\\operatorname{Spec}k</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> is faithfully flat between affines. The algebra <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k(t)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> is not finitely generated over <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> by Zariski’s lemma, since it is a transcendental field extension. Local finite presentation for this affine morphism would imply finite presentation.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/022F\">Stacks 022F · standard fpqc family</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topologies/#tag-022F\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/021M\">Stacks 021M · fppf family</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topologies/#tag-021M\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00FV\">Stacks 00FV · finite type field algebras</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00FV\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000111\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000111\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000113":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>A hypercover with two different edges over one vertex</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000846\"><span>Hypercover of an object of a site</span><code>P000846</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000847\"><span>Degree-zero covering of an object</span><code>P000847</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000848\"><span>Positive matching covers over an object</span><code>P000848</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000113\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000113</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000849\"><span>Čech hypercover of a covering family</span><code>P000849</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Augmented simplicial families over a site object</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> Use finite sets with finite jointly surjective families, over the singleton <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>. Set <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_0=X</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> and take <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_1</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> with two elements; both faces are the map to <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>, and choose one edge for the degeneracy. Take the relative <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>-coskeleton of this truncated object. The edge matching map is onto and all subsequent matching maps are isomorphisms. A Čech nerve with its single degree-zero vertex would have just one edge.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01G6\">Stacks 01G6 · numbered Stacks source and proof · Stacks 01G6 · an object hypercover outside the Čech subclass</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/hypercovering/#tag-01G6\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0205027#page=11\">Dugger–Hollander–Isaksen, Hypercovers and simplicial presheaves, Definition 4.10 and Lemma 4.11, height-zero hypercovers</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000113\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000113\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000114":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>Holomorphic exponential on the complex plane</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000875\"><span>Surjective morphism of sheaves of sets</span><code>P000875</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000114\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000114</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000876\"><span>Sectionwise surjectivity of a morphism of sheaves</span><code>P000876</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000874\"><span>Injective morphism of sheaves of sets</span><code>P000874</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Morphisms of sheaves of sets on a topological space</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> On the complex plane the exponential maps holomorphic functions to nowhere-vanishing holomorphic functions. Local logarithms give all local lifts. On the punctured plane the section <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>↦</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z\\mapsto z</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> has no global logarithm, since its logarithmic derivative <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/z</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> has nonzero integral around the unit circle. The constant sections <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> and <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi i</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> have the same image.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/007U\">Stacks 007U · local surjectivity of sheaves</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/sheaves/#tag-007U\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://math.stanford.edu/~vakil/216blog/FOAGfeb1724public.pdf\">R. Vakil, The Rising Sea, Example 2.4.9 (the holomorphic exponential)</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000114\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000114\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000115":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>Two overlapping principal opens whose coproduct is not an open immersion</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000950\"><span>Jointly covering family of open immersions</span><code>P000950</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000115\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000115</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000951\"><span>Coproduct map is an open immersion</span><code>P000951</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Families of open immersions over a scheme</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> On the affine line over a field the principal opens <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D(t)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> and <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D(1-t)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> cover. Their intersection contains the generic point. The coproduct maps two distinct points to that point, so its map is not an open immersion.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03FI\">Stacks 03FI · numbered assertion and proof · Stacks 03FI · (1) Zariski counterexample using the definition in 020O</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/descent/#tag-03FI\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000115\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000115\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000116":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>A nonflat test object detects failure of exactness</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000952\"><span>Right exact association to a big site</span><code>P000952</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000116\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000116</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000953\"><span>Left exact association to a big site</span><code>P000953</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Association of quasi-coherent modules to a big scheme site</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> Use <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S=\\operatorname{Spec}\\mathbb Z</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> in any of the indicated big sites. Association preserves cokernels. Multiplication by two on <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class=\"math-euler-script\" mathvariant=\"normal\">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal O_S</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> is injective, but on <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">𝔽</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Spec}\\mathbb F_2</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> it has a nonzero kernel.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/06VE\">Stacks 06VE · numbered assertion and proof · Stacks 06VE · (6) characteristic-two evaluation</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/descent/#tag-06VE\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000116\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000116\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000124":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>A real divisor with conjugate branches</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001023\"><span>Normal crossings divisor</span><code>P001023</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001014\"><span>Effective Cartier divisor on a locally Noetherian ambient scheme</span><code>P001014</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001019\"><span>Reduced closed subscheme</span><code>P001019</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000124\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000124</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001017\"><span>Strict normal crossings divisor</span><code>P001017</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Closed subschemes of locally Noetherian schemes</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> Take <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=V(x^2+y^2)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> in the affine plane over the reals. It is irreducible and singular at the origin, ruling out strict normal crossings there. Over the finite étale extension to the complex numbers it is the union of two distinct transverse lines.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BSF\">Stacks 0BSF · numbered source and proof · Stacks 0BSF · following definition: real conjugate branches</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/etale/#tag-0BSF\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000124\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000124\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000125":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>Strict normal crossings with singularities away from the divisor</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001017\"><span>Strict normal crossings divisor</span><code>P001017</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001023\"><span>Normal crossings divisor</span><code>P001023</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001015\"><span>Ambient regularity along the divisor</span><code>P001015</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000125\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000125</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001018\"><span>Regular ambient scheme</span><code>P001018</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Closed subschemes of locally Noetherian schemes</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> Take the disjoint union of the affine line over <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> and <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Spec}k[\\epsilon]/(\\epsilon^2)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>; let <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> be the origin in the line component. Its ideal is the unit ideal off that component, so it is an effective Cartier divisor with a regular parameter equation on its support. The ambient scheme, which would be the intersection of zero selected components, is singular on the second component.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BIA\">Stacks 0BIA · numbered source and proof · Stacks 0BIA · editorial boundary of the empty-intersection convention</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/etale/#tag-0BIA\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BI9\">Stacks 0BI9 · regularity is imposed along the divisor</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/etale/#tag-0BI9\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000125\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000125\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000127":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>The interval with finite proper covers excluded</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001059\"><span>Quasi-compact object of a site</span><code>P001059</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000127\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000127</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001060\"><span>Finite refinement of every cover</span><code>P001060</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001061\"><span>Finite covering subfamily of every cover</span><code>P001061</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Objects of a specified site</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> Take opens of the unit interval with identity covers and infinite indexed jointly covering families, retaining repetitions. Nonempty index sets ensure composites of infinite covers remain infinite. Compactness gives a finite subcover in the usual topology, which can be repeated infinitely to yield a covering refinement with finite image. An infinite cover by proper opens admits no finite allowed refinement since the only finite cover of the interval is its identity.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0D05\">Stacks 0D05 · numbered Stacks Project source · Stacks 0D05 · (1) does not imply (2)</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/sites/#tag-0D05\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000127\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000127\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000128":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>Four discrete points with singleton refinements</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001060\"><span>Finite refinement of every cover</span><code>P001060</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001059\"><span>Quasi-compact object of a site</span><code>P001059</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000128\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000128</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001061\"><span>Finite covering subfamily of every cover</span><code>P001061</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Objects of a specified site</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> On opens of a four-point discrete space admit identity covers, nonempty finite jointly covering families whose sources have at most one point, and countably infinite indexed jointly covering families. Use small chosen index sets closed under indexed composition. Pullbacks preserve these conditions. Composites are infinite if an infinite index occurs, and otherwise are identities or consist of opens of size at most one. Every cover refines by the points, using the identity for the empty target. An infinite cover alternating two complementary two-point opens has no admissible finite subfamily.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0D05\">Stacks 0D05 · numbered Stacks Project source · Stacks 0D05 · (2) does not imply (3), explicit witness</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/sites/#tag-0D05\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000128\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000128\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000129":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>The forgetful point of the topos of C₂-sets</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001071\"><span>Direct image of the point preserves all small limits</span><code>P001071</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000129\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000129</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001070\"><span>Direct image of the point preserves every coequalizer</span><code>P001070</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Geometric points of Grothendieck topoi</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> Take the point of the topos of left <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_2</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>-sets with inverse image the forgetful functor. Its right adjoint sends <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> to <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Map</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Map}(C_2,E)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> with the coinduced action. The parallel arrows <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>⇉</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\rightrightarrows\\{0,1\\}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> selecting the two distinct elements have singleton coequalizer. After applying the right adjoint the target has four functions and the two maps select the two constant functions. Coequalizing identifies only those two constants, leaving three elements, whereas the image of the singleton has one. Colimits of group actions are computed on underlying sets. Thus the coequalizer clause of 05UY fails in this generality.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/05UY\">Stacks 05UY · numbered Stacks Project source · Stacks 05UY · coequalizer clause fails for a general topos point</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/sites/#tag-05UY\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00W0\">Stacks 00W0 · the site presenting the topos of group actions</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/sites/#tag-00W0\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00Y9\">Stacks 00Y9 · formula for point direct image</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/sites/#tag-00Y9\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000129\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000129\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000130":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>Nonzero torsion on a square-zero thickening of a point</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001134\"><span>Affine scheme finitely presented over the prime field</span><code>P001134</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000130\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000130</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001132\"><span>Degree-zero crystalline cohomology without p-torsion</span><code>P001132</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001133\"><span>Injective Frobenius on degree-zero crystalline cohomology</span><code>P001133</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Crystalline cohomology with specified base and coefficients</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> Consider crystalline cohomology with structure-sheaf coefficients over <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">𝐙</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf Z_p</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> with the canonical divided powers on <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(p)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>. For <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">𝐅</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>y</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X=\\operatorname{Spec}(\\mathbf F_p[x,y]/(x,y)^2)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>, the closed class <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>y</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma_p(x^2)\\gamma_p(y^2)-\\gamma_p(xy)^2</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> survives evaluation in the explicit PD test algebra. Multiplication by <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> and pullback by Frobenius both kill it.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07LJ\">Stacks 07LJ · source statement and construction · Stacks 07LJ · (1) nonzero p-torsion class · Stacks 07LJ · (2) noninjective pullback by Frobenius</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/crystalline/#tag-07LJ\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1110.5001\">Bhatt–de Jong, Crystalline cohomology and de Rham cohomology, Example 3.11 (over the p-adic base in that paper)</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000130\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000130\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000131":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>Unbounded affine dimensions on a disconnected crystalline base</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001136\"><span>Quasi-compact base map and finite-type structure map</span><code>P001136</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000131\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000131</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001135\"><span>Uniform upper bound on crystalline higher direct images</span><code>P001135</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Crystalline cohomology with specified base and coefficients</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> Let the source and target bases both be the disjoint union of copies of Spec Fₚ with zero PD ideal, indexed by positive integers n. Over component n use affine n-space and map it to that point. These structure maps are finite type and quasi-separated even on the disjoint union, since quasi-compact opens of the base meet finitely many components. Evaluating the crystalline structure sheaf at the trivial thickening on component n computes ordinary de Rham cohomology in characteristic p. The top form with coefficient the product of the (p−1)st powers of all n coordinates cannot be exact: differentiation in any one coordinate gives coefficient zero at this monomial. Thus the direct images have unbounded nonzero degrees globally.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07MQ\">Stacks 07MQ · source statement and construction · Stacks 07MQ · editorial counterexample to global uniformity</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/crystalline/#tag-07MQ\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07MW\">Stacks 07MW · smooth lifting comparison</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/crystalline/#tag-07MW\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07LH\">Stacks 07LH · affine calculation for the smooth presentation</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/crystalline/#tag-07LH\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07MJ\">Stacks 07MJ · evaluation of the direct image</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/crystalline/#tag-07MJ\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000131\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000131\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000132":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>A punctured valuation spectrum with no closed points</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000013\"><span>Reduced</span><code>P000013</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000028\"><span>Irreducible</span><code>P000028</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000132\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000132</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001166\"><span>Scheme with at least one closed point</span><code>P001166</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> Take a valuation domain with value group <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℤ</mi><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Gamma=\\bigoplus_{i\\geq1}\\mathbb Z e_i</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>, ordered by the first nonzero coordinate. For instance use the least monomial exponent valuation on <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k(t_1,t_2,\\ldots)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>. The convex subgroups are <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> and the tails <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℤ</mi><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_n=\\bigoplus_{i&gt;n}\\mathbb Z e_i</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> for <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\geq0</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>: the least leading index of any nonzero convex subgroup determines that tail. The primes corresponding to the <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_n</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> are the elements of positive value modulo <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_n</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>, together with zero. They form a strictly increasing sequence from the zero ideal, whose union is the maximal ideal. Removing the latter leaves a nonempty irreducible reduced scheme in which every point has a distinct specialization. This corrects the printed cuts: <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e_i-e_{i+1}&lt;e_i</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> while <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2(e_i-e_{i+1})&gt;e_i</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>, so <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e_i+\\Gamma_{\\geq0}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> does not define a prime.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01IY\">Stacks 01IY · numbered Stacks Project source · Stacks 01IY · nonempty example with no closed point</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/schemes/#tag-01IY\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00IH\">Stacks 00IH · ideals of valuation domains and their value groups</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00IH\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000132\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000132\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000133":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>Infinite-dimensional affine space punctured at the origin</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000546\"><span>Open immersion</span><code>P000546</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000133\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000133</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000022\"><span>Quasi-compact</span><code>P000022</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Morphisms of schemes</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> For <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X=\\operatorname{Spec}k[x_1,x_2,\\ldots]</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> remove the closed origin defined by all variables. The resulting open immersion has non-quasi-compact source: the <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D(x_i)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> cover it and any finite list misses the prime ideal generated by those listed variables. The target itself is quasi-compact.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01K8\">Stacks 01K8 · numbered Stacks Project source · Stacks 01K8 · open immersion need not be quasi-compact</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/schemes/#tag-01K8\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000133\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000133\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000134":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>An immersion with no open-then-closed factorization</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000548\"><span>Immersion</span><code>P000548</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000134\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000134</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001167\"><span>Open immersion followed by a closed immersion</span><code>P001167</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Morphisms of schemes</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> Use <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X=\\operatorname{Spec}k[x_1,x_2,\\ldots]</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> and <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>⋃</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=\\bigcup D(x_n)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>. On <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D(x_n)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> take the ideal generated by <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_i^n</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> for <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i&lt;n</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>, by <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_n-1</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>, and by the <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_j</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> for <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j&gt;n</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>. On a two-chart overlap both extended ideals are the unit ideal, so the closed subschemes glue inside <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>. The map of their union into <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> is an immersion. Any polynomial vanishing on every piece is zero: choose <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> beyond all its variables and degrees and reduce in the truncated polynomial algebra on that component. Thus a containing closed subscheme would be all of <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>, where this union is not open.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01IP\">Stacks 01IP · numbered Stacks Project source · Stacks 01IP · failure of the reversed factorization</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/schemes/#tag-01IP\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01QW\">Stacks 01QW · explicit construction over a polynomial ring in countably many variables</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/morphisms/#tag-01QW\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000134\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000134\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000135":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>Connected affine normal scheme with several components</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000031\"><span>Affine</span><code>P000031</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000012\"><span>Normal</span><code>P000012</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000029\"><span>Connected</span><code>P000029</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000135\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000135</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000028\"><span>Irreducible</span><code>P000028</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> The example linked from 033O has a connected spectrum, several minimal primes and domain stalks. Its normality is recorded separately in the remark with proof omitted.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/033O\">Stacks 033O · example and its stated normality · Stacks 033O · connected example with reducible spectrum</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-033O\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000135\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000135\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000136":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>A section over a principal open outside the Hom image</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001183\"><span>Finitely generated specified ideal</span><code>P001183</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000136\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000136</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001184\"><span>Surjectivity from ideal-power Hom modules</span><code>P001184</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Rings with an ideal and a module</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> For a field k take A = k[x,y,z₁,z₂,…]/(xⁿzₙ : n ≥ 1), I = (x), M = A. The section y/x over D(x) cannot come from a map Iⁿ → A. Clearing denominators would give xʳ⁻¹ y zᵣ = 0 for some r, although this monomial survives.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01PN\">Stacks 01PN · first example</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-01PN\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000136\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000136\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000137":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>Compatible local fractions outside the Hom image</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001183\"><span>Finitely generated specified ideal</span><code>P001183</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000137\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000137</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001184\"><span>Surjectivity from ideal-power Hom modules</span><code>P001184</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Rings with an ideal and a module</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> Take A = k[f,g,x,y,a₁,b₁,…]/(fy−gx, aₙfⁿ+bₙgⁿ : n ≥ 1), I = (f,g), M = A. The fractions x/f and y/g glue on D(f) ∪ D(g). Applying a hypothetical lifting map to the displayed relation at sufficiently high degree yields the nonzero element aᵣfʳ⁻¹x+bᵣgʳ⁻¹y.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01PN\">Stacks 01PN · second example</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-01PN\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000137\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000137\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000138":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>Two component generic points without a common affine</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000033\"><span>Quasi-compact</span><code>P000033</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000138\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000138</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000035\"><span>Quasi-separated</span><code>P000035</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001188\"><span>Common affine neighbourhood for finite sets of component generic points</span><code>P001188</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> For R = ℚ[x,y₁,y₂,…]/((x−i)yᵢ : i ≥ 1), double Spec R along the complement of the vanishing set of (y₁,y₂,…). The common open is not quasi-compact. Any neighbourhood of the specified minimal prime sees infinitely many shared lines, and the two resulting generic points cannot lie in one affine. Two affine opens cover the double.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01ZW\">Stacks 01ZW · doubled-spectrum counterexample</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-01ZW\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000138\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000138\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000139":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>Excellent stalks with nonfinite normalization</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001195\"><span>Japanese domain stalks</span><code>P001195</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000037\"><span>Integral</span><code>P000037</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000036\"><span>Noetherian</span><code>P000036</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000139\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000139</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001173\"><span>Japanese integral scheme</span><code>P001173</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Schemes</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> Take the one-dimensional Noetherian domain cited in the remark. Its local rings are excellent domains, hence Japanese. Its normalization in its own fraction field is not finite, so the affine scheme is not Japanese by 033V.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/033T\">Stacks 033T · counterexample to determination by Japanese local rings</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/properties/#tag-033T\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000139\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000139\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000140":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>A flat surjective singleton over a non-quasi-separated target</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001107\"><span>Flatness of every arrow</span><code>P001107</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001109\"><span>Joint surjectivity</span><code>P001109</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001196\"><span>Pointwise neighbourhoods given by quasi-compact images</span><code>P001196</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000140\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000140</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001197\"><span>Quasi-separated target of a family</span><code>P001197</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P001108\"><span>Finite affine image lifting</span><code>P001108</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000832\"><span>fpqc covering family</span><code>P000832</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Families of morphisms with a common target</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> Let <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=k[t_1,t_2,\\ldots]</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> for a field <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> and let <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">𝔪</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathfrak m=(t_i\\mid i\\ge1)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>. Glue two copies <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X_1,X_2</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> of <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Spec}A</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> away from <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">𝔪</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathfrak m</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>. Their intersection is not quasi-compact. The singleton from <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⨿</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">𝔪</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y=X_1\\amalg\\operatorname{Spec}A_{\\mathfrak m}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> uses the first chart and the localization in the second chart. It is flat and onto, so the quasi-compact open <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> supplies every pointwise neighbourhood. A quasi-compact open mapping onto exactly <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X_2</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> would meet <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X_1</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> in finitely many principal opens <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D(f_j)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> with <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">𝔪</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_j\\in\\mathfrak m</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>. Choose an index <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> absent from all <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_j</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>. The closed point with <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_r=1</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> and every other coordinate zero lies in none of these opens and is outside the image of <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">𝔪</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Spec}A_{\\mathfrak m}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>. Even a finite union of affine opens therefore cannot have image exactly <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X_2</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0H7E\">Stacks 0H7E · flat surjection satisfying (4) which fails the fpqc test · Stacks 0H7E · positive pointwise criterion and failure of the affine fpqc test</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topologies/#tag-0H7E\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01KL\">Stacks 01KL · doubled origin with non-quasi-compact intersection</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/schemes/#tag-01KL\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/022B\">Stacks 022B · finite affine image criterion</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topologies/#tag-022B\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000140\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000140\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000104":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>An fpqc covering sieve with no fppf covering family</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000755\"><span>Fpqc covering sieve on the big site</span><code>P000755</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000104\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000104</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000754\"><span>Fppf covering sieve on the big site</span><code>P000754</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Sieves on the big category of schemes</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> For any field <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> use the sieve generated by <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Spec} k(t)\\to\\operatorname{Spec} k</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>. This affine faithfully flat morphism covers for fpqc. If a nonempty locally finitely presented <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>-scheme factored through it, a nonempty affine neighbourhood of finite type would have a closed point with residue field finite over <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>. The factorization would embed <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k(t)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> in that field over <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>, a contradiction.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/022A\">Stacks 022A · fpqc coverings</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topologies/#tag-022A\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/021N\">Stacks 021N · fppf coverings</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topologies/#tag-021N\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00FV\">Stacks 00FV · residue fields at maximal ideals of finite type algebras</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00FV\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000104\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000104\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000103":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>A totally separated space without a clopen basis</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000749\"><span>Totally separated space</span><code>P000749</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000053\"><span>Hausdorff space</span><code>P000053</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000103\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000103</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000064\"><span>Clopen basis (point-set zero dimensionality)</span><code>P000064</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000055\"><span>Profinite space</span><code>P000055</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Topological spaces</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> Take the subspace <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">𝐑</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">𝐐</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">𝐍</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=\\ell^2(\\mathbf R)\\cap\\mathbf Q^{\\mathbf N}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> with the norm topology. Distinct sequences differ in some coordinate. Cutting that coordinate at an irrational number strictly between the two values gives a clopen separation. Erdős proved that this separable metrizable space has dimension one, so it cannot have a clopen basis. Consequently it is not profinite.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://www.renyi.hu/~p_erdos/1940-11.pdf\">P. Erdős, The dimension of the rational points in Hilbert space (1940), pp. 734–736</a> · <a href=\"https://topology.pi-base.org/spaces/S000142\">π-Base S000142 · Erdős space</a> · <a href=\"https://topology.pi-base.org/properties/P000048\">π-Base P000048 · Totally separated</a> · <a href=\"https://topology.pi-base.org/properties/P000050\">π-Base P000050 · Clopen basis</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/08ZY\">Stacks 08ZY · profinite spaces and finite discrete quotients</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topology/#tag-08ZY\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000103\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000103\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000105":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>An injective map of spectra with two points after base change</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000772\"><span>Injective on underlying points</span><code>P000772</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000019\"><span>Finite</span><code>P000019</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000043\"><span>Surjective</span><code>P000043</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000105\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000105</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000044\"><span>Universally injective</span><code>P000044</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000773\"><span>Purely inseparable residue extensions everywhere</span><code>P000773</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Morphisms of schemes</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> Both <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Spec}\\mathbb C</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> and <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Spec}\\mathbb R</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> have one point. The residue extension is separable of degree two. Base change to <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb C</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> gives <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℂ</mi><msub><mo>⊗</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℝ</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℂ</mi><mo>≅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℂ</mi><mo>×</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb C\\otimes_{\\mathbb R}\\mathbb C\\cong\\mathbb C\\times\\mathbb C</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>, with the two factors indexed by the identity and complex conjugation. Both source points map to the unique target point.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01S4\">Stacks 01S4 · criteria for universal injectivity</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/morphisms/#tag-01S4\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0CKN\">Stacks 0CKN · splitting a finite separable extension after scalar extension</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/fields/#tag-0CKN\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://www.jmilne.org/math/CourseNotes/FT.pdf#page=107\">J. S. Milne, Fields and Galois Theory, v5.10, proof of Proposition 8.6(c) ⇒ (a), applied to the two roots of the minimal polynomial of i</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000105\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000105\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>","X000106":"<article class=\"algebra-card\" data-algebra-kind=\"example\" data-relation-style=\"example\"><header><span class=\"algebra-type\">Counterexample or example</span><h3>A finite surjective morphism flat on just one component</h3></header><div class=\"algebra-sequent\"><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Holds in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000019\"><span>Finite</span><code>P000019</code></a><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000043\"><span>Surjective</span><code>P000043</code></a></div><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">─</span><a href=\"../database/#X000106\" class=\"algebra-relation-code\" title=\"Open shared record\">X000106</a><span class=\"algebra-stroke\" aria-hidden=\"true\">∥</span><div class=\"algebra-lane\"><span class=\"algebra-lane-title\">Fails in this example</span><a class=\"algebra-node\" href=\"#P000006\"><span>Flat</span><code>P000006</code></a></div></div><p class=\"property-context\">Domain: Morphisms of schemes</p><p><strong>Witness.</strong> Fix a prime <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>. The map <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℤ</mi><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℤ</mi><mo>×</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">𝔽</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb Z\\to\\mathbb Z\\times\\mathbb F_p</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> induces a finite surjective morphism. On the first component it is the identity. At the point of the second component the local ring map is <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℤ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">𝔽</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb Z_{(p)}\\to\\mathbb F_p</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>. Multiplication by <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> is injective on <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℤ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb Z_{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>. Tensoring with <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">𝔽</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb F_p</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> turns this injection into the zero endomorphism of a nonzero module. Thus flatness holds at every source point of the first component and fails at the point of the second.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01U3\">Stacks 01U3 · flatness at a point and at every point</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/morphisms/#tag-01U3\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00HD\">Stacks 00HD · flat modules preserve injections</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/algebra/#tag-00HD\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-658-30733-2_15\">U. Görtz and T. Wedhorn, Algebraic Geometry I, second edition, Definition 14.1(1),(3), testing the two local ring maps independently</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"#X000106\">Permalink</a> · <a href=\"../database/#X000106\">Shared catalogue</a></p></article>"};
